#
# Per-rig device configuration for the input subprocess.
#
# Sourced from config/inputconf.tcl AFTER the built-in known-device table
# is seeded (wch / ILITEK / eGalax touchscreens) and BEFORE
# inputAutodiscover runs. So local entries override the defaults
# (last-match-wins) and new entries added here are visible to discovery.
#
# On a rig with only the standard known hardware attached, this file is
# not needed — autodiscover just works. Use it to:
#
#   - teach the module about new hardware (inputKnownDevice)
#   - name the dedicated mouse this rig answers with (see below)
#   - declare what devices this rig must have (inputExpect)
#   - override a default rotation or screen size per-rig
#
# SCOPE — this file runs in the INPUT subprocess, which has no `ess`
# package. Any ::ess::* command here fails and takes the whole input
# config with it: no autodiscover, no devices, and an input page that is
# simply empty. Device DISCOVERY is this file's job. Which transport
# answers a response mode is ess's, and lives in local/post-input.tcl
# (::ess::dial_bind, ::ess::button_bind, ::ess::joystick_bind).
#
# COMPANION — local/input_devices.tcl is written by the input page's
# "save for this rig" and sourced AFTER this file, so a page-saved entry
# wins over one written here. That file is machine-owned: do not hand-edit
# it. Adopting and saving from the page is usually easier than writing an
# entry below, and it fills in the extent for you.
#

# --- Expectations ------------------------------------------------------
#
# By default, startup is permissive: missing devices are ignored.
# Declaring `inputExpect` makes the rig fail loudly at startup if the
# class has no matched device. `-optional` is available if you want the
# declaration for documentation / diagnostics but no enforcement.
#
# inputExpect touchscreen
# inputExpect mouse
# inputExpect trackpad -optional

# --- A dedicated mouse (the one class that REQUIRES an entry) -----------
#
# Touchscreens and trackpads auto-enable on capability match. A mouse
# never does: on a rig with a desktop, the first EV_REL device udev
# enumerates is as likely to be the operator's mouse as the subject's,
# and adopting that one with -grab would take the desktop pointer away
# with no obvious cause. So a mouse is adopted only if an entry names it.
#
# Find the pattern with `send input "inputProbe"` or `ls /dev/input/by-id`,
# and match the subject's device only. Two identical mice share a by-id
# name — use by-path (physical port) if you ever need to tell them apart.
#
# inputKnownDevice mouse *Logitech_USB_Optical_Mouse* \
#     -screen_w 1920 -screen_h 1080 -gain 1.0 -grab 1 -move_every 5
#
# Options:
#   -screen_w W       extent the virtual cursor is clamped to. A mouse is
#   -screen_h H       relative; these define the space it moves in, and
#                     usually match the stimulus display.
#   -gain G           counts -> pixels, LINEAR (no acceleration curve, so
#                     the same hand movement always travels the same
#                     distance and can be stated in a methods section)
#   -grab 0|1         EVIOCGRAB: the kernel stops delivering this device
#                     to anyone else, so the desktop pointer does not
#                     follow the subject. Released when dserv exits.
#   -move_every N     publish only every Nth MOTION sample. Button
#                     press/release are never decimated, so click timing
#                     keeps the device's full resolution. Use this rather
#                     than capping usbhid.mousepoll, which would coarsen
#                     the button timestamps too.
#
# RATE — Raspberry Pi OS caps HID polling at ~62.5 Hz. A 1 kHz mouse will
# report at 62.5 Hz until usbhid.mousepoll=1 is on the kernel command line
# (/boot/firmware/cmdline.txt; usbhid is built in, so modprobe.d does NOT
# apply). The input page shows the current setting on every adopted
# device — if it is not 0 or 1, the rate you measure is the setting rather
# than the hardware.

# --- Teaching the module about new hardware ----------------------------
#
# Touchscreens: must provide screen_w and screen_h. Match against the
# /dev/input/by-id/<name> basename (Tcl glob) or the libevdev-reported
# device name.
#
# inputKnownDevice touchscreen *MyNewPanel_USB_1024x600* \
#     -screen_w 1024 -screen_h 600
#
# Options:
#   -screen_w W       pixel width of the display this panel covers
#   -screen_h H       pixel height
#   -rotation N       0 / 90 / 180 / 270 (default -1 = auto-detect from
#                     /proc/cmdline's video=HDMI-A-1:...:rotate=N)
#   -track_drag 0|1   emit DRAG events on movement while active
#                     (default 0: only PRESS and RELEASE)
#   -hdmi_output name HDMI connector name for rotation detection
#                     (default HDMI-A-1)
#   -grab 0|1         also available here: hide the panel from the desktop
#
# Trackpads need no entry at all — they auto-enable and publish their own
# surface range. Add one only to set -grab, or to pin a specific device.

# --- Overriding a built-in default -------------------------------------
#
# To override the built-in defaults for a known panel on this specific
# rig (e.g. this ILITEK is mounted rotated), re-declare the same pattern
# with different values. Last-match-wins.
#
# inputKnownDevice touchscreen *ILITEK_ILITEK-TP* \
#     -screen_w 1280 -screen_h 800 -rotation 180

# --- Debugging discovery -----------------------------------------------
#
# inputProbe enumerates every /dev/input/event* with identifiers, the
# classifier's verdict, and capability bits. Invoke on demand:
#
#   send input "inputProbe"
#
# Or at startup by setting DSERV_INPUT_PROBE=1 in the environment / unit.
#
# The input page shows the same information with live events, which is
# usually the faster way in: it lists what was NOT adopted and why, and
# lets you try a device before committing it to this file.
